<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1181–1297 (from Jayavarman VII to Zhou Daguan's stay at Angkor)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</h1><p class="soc-sub">From God-King to Slave</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bayon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayon.jpg" alt="Bayon"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a><span class="soc-credit">Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> society was arranged in a hierarchy that reflected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system. At its head stood a king whom the cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">devaraja</a>, the “god-king”, identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, or under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> (r. 1181–1219) with the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, and whose temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a>, celebrated his divine rule on earth. Below him the kshatriyas, royalty, nobles and warriors, formed the governing elite, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> served as priests and often as high officials. Traders and artisans worked in and for the cities, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers and fishermen made up the large majority, and their harvests, watered by a vast network of canals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, fed a capital region of some 700,000 to 900,000 people in the 13th century. Slaves stood at the bottom. No written records of the period survive other than stone inscriptions, so much of what is known of ordinary lives comes from the reliefs of the Bayon and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a>, a Chinese envoy who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> from August 1296 to July 1297.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1181–1297 (from Jayavarman VII to Zhou Daguan's stay at Angkor)</span><span><b>People</b> c. 2–4 million (est.)</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 8</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Devaraja</span> <span class="soc-en">(The king)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Kshatriya</span> <span class="soc-en">(Royalty, ministers and nobles)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Brahmins</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.1–0.5% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Buddhist monks</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:6%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Traders and artisans</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 3–8% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:80%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Rice farmers and fishermen</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 80% (the rest, est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-dependent soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Temple servants</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:10%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Slaves</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 5–15% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jayavarman-vii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Devaraja</span> <span class="soc-en">(The king)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">chakravartin, kamraten jagad ta raja</p><p class="soc-kid">The kings of Angkor were seen as gods on earth, and they built huge stone temples such as Angkor Wat and the Bayon.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> proclaimed himself chakravartin, “universal ruler”, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">devaraja</a>, “god-king”, in 802, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> kings were held to have the divine quality of living gods, incarnations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. At the heart of a royal city stood a temple-mountain whose central lingam bore the king’s own name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> Buddhist, represented himself instead as the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara in the face towers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a>. The king and his officials ran the canals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> on which the harvests depended, and kings led large armies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> and Dai Viet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> saw the king ride out standing on an elephant whose tusks were encased in gold, holding his sacred sword.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> No settled rule governed the succession: of the 27 rulers of Kambuja, eleven lacked a legitimate claim to power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-i">Suryavarman I</a> took the throne by seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> by retaking it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>, and in 1295 Jayavarman VIII was deposed by his son-in-law Srindravarman.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One king, with the wives and concubines who rode behind him in procession under more than a hundred gold-flecked parasols.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">Devaraja</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Kshatriya</span> <span class="soc-en">(Royalty, ministers and nobles)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Princes and ministers rode on elephants under red umbrellas, and the higher a man’s rank, the bigger his house.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The kshatriyas, royalty, nobles, warlords, soldiers and warriors, formed the governing elite. The king was surrounded by ministers, state officials, nobles and royal kin, and in his procession <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> saw the ministers and princes mounted on elephants under innumerable red umbrellas. Rank was written into their homes: the dwellings of the princes and principal officials were much larger than those of the people and laid out differently, their size set by each person’s official rank, and only the family temple and the main apartment could be roofed with tiles. Officials managed the waterworks and collected a rent for each trader’s place in the market.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Royal and noble birth gave entry to the rank, but its members fought over it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> kings and royal families were often caught up in power struggles over the succession or rivalries over principalities, and the relationship between the rulers and their elites was unstable. A prince could rise as a soldier, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> did, leading armies under earlier kings before he took the throne.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: no count survives; the royal family, the ministers, officials and nobles and their households, a governing elite among 2–4 million people.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The procession of Suryavarman II, a relief at Angkor Wat · Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat_-_065_Suryavarman_Procession_(8581685668).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-3"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 0.1–0.5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Brahmins</span></h3><p class="soc-term">brahmin</p><p class="soc-kid">Brahmins were Hindu priests who led the ceremonies of the king and the nobles.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> was the state religion for most of the empire’s history, and its ceremonies and rituals were performed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, usually only among the king’s family, the nobles and the ruling class. The rite by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> made himself god-king in 802 was taken from the Hindu tradition, and the royal cult was housed in temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, from Pre Rup to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> had little to say about the Brahmans, except that they were often employed as high officials. Beside them he saw a third group, whom he called by a Chinese name and who appear to have been Shaivites: the only image they revered, he wrote, was a block of stone.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The priests’ fortunes followed the king’s faith. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> the state religion and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples altered to show the Buddha; from about 1243 Jayavarman VIII restored Hinduism, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and replaced Buddha images with lingams. From the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> prevailed, and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscription dates from 1327.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: no count survives; a priestly class whose Hindu rites were held mostly among the king’s family, the nobles and the ruling class, small beside the farming majority.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Shiva dancing, a relief of the 10th century at Banteay Srei · Pierre André Leclercq · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reliefs_of_Shiva_Dancing_(Banteay_Srei).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Buddhist monks</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Buddhist monks at Angkor shaved their heads and wore yellow robes.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">For most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> came after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> in popularity, though kings built Buddhist temples and monasteries beside their Hindu shrines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> for his mother and Preah Khan for his father. During the 13th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, which had come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> by way of the kingdoms of Dvaravati and Haripunchai, spread even among the lower classes. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> arrived in 1296 it was the dominant religion: its monks had shaven heads and wore yellow robes, and its temples impressed him with their simplicity, their Buddha images made of gilded plaster. Srindravarman, who took the throne in 1295, was a follower of Theravada Buddhism.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The surviving sources say little of how men entered or left the monkhood. The faith’s spread mattered to the whole order: George Coedès argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a>’s denial of the ultimate reality of the individual sapped the royal cult that had raised the great temples.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: no count survives; by 1296 Theravada Buddhism was the dominant religion and its monks a common sight, but how many there were is not known.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Cambodian Buddhist monks, 1860s · Emile Gsell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pics_397639_VF_273_Cambodian_Khmer_Buddhist_monks_Buddhism_Gsell.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 3–8% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Traders and artisans</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">At Angkor’s market most of the traders were women, who sat on straw mats on the ground to sell their goods.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Among the other classes were traders and artisans: carpenters and stonemasons, potters, metalworkers, goldsmiths and textile weavers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> was the preferred material for statues of the gods. The market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> had no permanent buildings: it was an open square where traders sat on woven straw mats on the ground, some under a thatched parasol, and officials levied a rent for each space. Until the 13th century barter was the main way of exchange; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, grain and Chinese coins for small purchases, cloth as another medium, and gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for the largest. Trade was mainly in women’s hands. No local people produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, Zhou wrote, and they wove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> from kapok by hand, without a loom.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Newcomers brought crafts of their own: settlers from Siam grew mulberry trees, raised silkworms and wove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and the local people asked Siamese women to mend their clothes. A Chinese man arriving at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, Zhou wrote, first took in a local woman, partly to profit from her skill in trade.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: no count survives; the farmers were the large majority, and a capital region of 700,000–900,000 people needed its carpenters, stonemasons, potters, metalworkers, goldsmiths, weavers and market traders.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A market scene in the reliefs of the Bayon · Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayonmarket01.JPG" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 80% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Rice farmers and fishermen</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Most Khmer people were rice farmers and fishermen who lived in bamboo houses on stilts, safe from floods.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> farmers and fishermen formed the large majority. They planted rice by the banks of the lake and rivers, in the irrigated plains around their villages, or in the hills when the lowlands flooded, and canals and great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, the barays, watered their fields. Orchards gave palm sugar, coconuts, fruit and vegetables. Fish was the main source of protein, often made into prahok, a fish paste; pigs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and poultry were kept beneath houses of woven bamboo raised on stilts against the floods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> saw peasant men and women go barefoot with bare chests, wearing only a cloth around the waist. The reliefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> show them fishing, butchering, playing a chess-like game and gambling on cockfights.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> War reached them all: when the Siamese attacked, Zhou wrote, ordinary people were ordered to face them without strategy or preparation, and soldiers fought naked and barefoot with lance and shield. When droughts and floods damaged the waterworks in the 14th and 15th centuries, many may have left the great cities and moved south.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other ranks: the large majority of the people. The empire’s population and several other ranks are estimates, so this share is one too.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Rice fields around Angkor Thom · diego_cue · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rice_fields_around_Angkor_Thom_-_Cambodia_-_panoramio.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-dependent" id="rank-7"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ta-prohm.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Serfs and dependants</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Temple servants</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Giant temples like Ta Prohm needed thousands of people to look after them.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Only the gods had a right to houses of stone, so the temples were the empire’s great buildings, and they needed a great workforce. A stone carving at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a>, the monastery <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> founded for his mother, states that 12,640 people serviced that single temple complex, and one estimate puts at some 300,000 the agricultural workers needed to feed the temples’ servants. Most surviving stone inscriptions record the religious foundations of kings and other potentates, so the temples’ people are known mostly as numbers. In the reign of Jayavarman VII alone Ta Prohm, Preah Khan, Banteay Kdei and Neak Pean were built, beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> at the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The surviving sources used here do not say how people came to serve a temple, or whether they could leave. George Coedès argued that the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> wore away the royal cult that had raised the temples, and the workforce that served them may have shrunk with it.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: an inscription at Ta Prohm counts 12,640 people serving that one temple, and the Angkor area holds over a thousand temples; how many served temples full time across the empire is not known.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-8"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/angkor-thom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>c. 5–15% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Slaves</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">At the bottom of Khmer society were slaves, people who belonged to others and were not free.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Slaves stood on the lowest social level of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> society, beneath the farmers and fishermen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a>’s account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, some forty pages long, treats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> among its subjects beside religion, justice, kingship, agriculture and commerce, and with the inscriptions and the reliefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> it is an important source on everyday life in the city. What he wrote of slaves, how many there were and what work they did, however, the accounts of his book used here do not record. Zhou did note that the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, open by day and closed at night, were barred to dogs and to convicts.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> How people became slaves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, and whether they could go free, is not recorded in the sources used here.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: no count survives; slaves stood on the lowest level of society, and the share given here is a wide guess for want of any figure.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="khmer-empire">Be born in the Khmer Empire</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Birth set most people’s place, and the sources record movement chiefly at the top. The throne itself changed hands by force again and again: eleven of the 27 rulers of Kambuja lacked a legitimate claim, and kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-i">Suryavarman I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> won it by taking the capital. Faith could lift or lower whole groups: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>’ rites and the Buddhist monasteries rose and fell with the religion of each king, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> spread in the 13th century even among the lower classes. Foreigners found places of their own: Siamese settlers wove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and Chinese men took local women and shared in their trade. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> declined its people moved too: the court left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> area in 1431, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, which took over Angkor’s social stratification, the scribes, court Brahmans, jurists, chamberlains, accountants, physicians and astrologers of the early kingdom usually came from Khmer-speaking elites.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Women ran most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>’s trade. “The local people who know how to trade are all women,” <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> wrote, and the role suggests that they enjoyed significant rights and freedom. They married and bore children young, which may have added to the kingdom’s large population, and Zhou, judging by Chinese eyes, thought women of twenty or thirty looked like Chinese women of forty or fifty. Beautiful women were sent to court to serve the king or his family at his whim: in the royal procession three to five hundred palace women with flowers in their hair carried candles, others the royal regalia of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and others lances and shields, ahead of the king’s guards, while his wives and concubines followed in palanquins and carriages and on horses and elephants. Common women wore a strip of cloth across the chest, noblewomen a longer one over the shoulder, and common women wore hair ornaments and gold rings or bracelets. In a farmer’s house the daughters had a room of their own, while the sons slept wherever they found space. Royal women were honoured in stone: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> for his mother.</p></section>
