<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Cœdès, G. (1966) The making of South East Asia. University of California Press.</li><li>Higham, C. (2001) The Civilization of Angkor. Phoenix.</li><li>Freeman, M. and Jacques, C. (2006) Ancient Angkor. River Books.</li><li>Dagens, B. (1995) Angkor: Heart of an Asian Empire. Translated by Ruth Sharman. London: Thames &amp; Hudson.</li><li>Snellgrove, D. L. (2004) Angkor, Before and After: A Cultural History of the Khmers. Orchid Press.</li><li>Zhou Daguan (2007) The Customs of Cambodia. The Siam Society.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate: the Greater Angkor Region alone held some 700,000–900,000 people at its peak in the 13th century, and the empire ruled or held as vassals most of mainland Southeast Asia beyond it; no figure for the whole empire rests on a count.</p></section>
